**What came in this turn:** plat's A6-fix PR (`ddae842`), then a cascade of crossed verdicts as a defect I found triggered a multi-party oscillation.

**The substance:** On adversarial re-test of QA's "cosmetic" flag, I reproduced **D4** — the `__rq{n}` re-wake counter isn't monotonic (`n=len(glob)` recounts surviving files), so on the **3rd consecutive zero-commit failure** the re-wake collides with the in-flight touch and self-cancels → the autonomous doorbell dies under strict zero-traffic.

**The resolution (ORCH binding, `A6_FINAL_NONBLOCKING_FULL_BUNDLE`):** **A6 ACCEPTED non-blocking.** The correct principled basis (SEC's, ORCH-ratified, code-verified at `agent_reactor.py:380`): the anti-silent-consumption test is **observability via the `failed_no_commit` ledger** — D1 had no ledger (truly silent → blocking); D4 writes one every cycle (auditable → *not* silent → A6's intent is met). D4's counter → non-blocking backlog. ORCH issued the **full-bundle merge GoDecision** (OPT-3 + OPT-5 + A6 + OPT-1a shadow); plat lands fleet-opt commits only (PM-confirmed clean separation from ProdKit WS5) and reports the landed sha.

**My conduct — owned plainly:** I flipped the verdict ~5 times as crossed messages raced, which was the wrong way to handle a high-churn dispute. I've recorded the outcome in [[epic-fleet-optimization]] and banked the lesson in [[lesson-anchor-on-arbiter-newest-ruling]]: anchor on the arbiter's newest ruling by sequence, don't re-flip on stale peer replies, escalate AC-scope once and hold, and judge "silent consumption" by observability rather than durability.

